Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["EuclideanGeometryGuide", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Euclidean geometry rebuilt inside a graph. The graph is all there is: no \
ambient space, no coordinates. Each Euclidean notion is redefined using graph \
properties alone, and the shortest-path metric is the layer these definitions \
sit on. Two things change from the plane. A construction returns a ",
 StyleBox["set", "TI"],
 " of admissible answers rather than one, so uniqueness fails generically. \
And some objects fail to exist at all \[LongDash] a circle of a single radius \
is empty on a lattice, and a perpendicular bisector is empty at odd distance. \
Both are results, not defects."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

